@broadcast "Raptor News Network: Nightly Fury"
@channel news1
@category news
@type news
@anchor "Brick Hardline"
@anchor "Chastity Vale"
@reporter "Ronnie Vasquez"
@reporter "Dallas Cru"
@meteorologist "Skip Vandermeer"
@announcer "The Voice of Raptor"
@length 200
@titlecard rnn_title
@theme "Theme Jingle 1"

# =============================================================================
# RAPTOR NEWS NETWORK — a Fox-News-parody nightly bulletin for the Coldwater Basin.
# There is no game state to read: the news runner pulls the SAME dynamic stories the
# tablet's News app shows (via the news generator) and reads them out through the
# anchors and field reporters below — one random line per matching pool, {tokens}
# filled from each story and the show's cast. A fresh bulletin re-rolls as the world's
# news does. The anchors/reporters/announcer are NAMES, not NPCs — no studio host is
# ever spawned. See docs/bsm-format.md#news-broadcasts-type-news.
#
# Tokens: {anchor} {anchor2} {reporter} {announcer} {scene} {headline} {body} {byline}
# The weather segment (the wx.* pools, below the rundown) is the one part of the bulletin
# with REAL facts in it: it reads the same live 7-day forecast DOOMCAST does, so the sky
# Skip describes is the sky outside. See the block comment above those pools.
# =============================================================================

# --- Show logo / title card: shown once before the bulletin each airing (@titlecard) -----
# A raptor-eye roundel emblem in a red broadcast burst + the RAPTOR wordmark lockup.
::asset rnn_title
<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 640 360" font-family="'Segoe UI',Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif">
  <defs>
    <linearGradient id="rnnbg" x1="0" y1="0" x2="0" y2="1">
      <stop offset="0" stop-color="#1a0407"/><stop offset="0.55" stop-color="#0d0203"/><stop offset="1" stop-color="#040101"/>
    </linearGradient>
    <radialGradient id="rnnglow" cx="0.28" cy="0.4" r="0.62">
      <stop offset="0" stop-color="#e11d2a" stop-opacity="0.30"/><stop offset="1" stop-color="#e11d2a" stop-opacity="0"/>
    </radialGradient>
    <linearGradient id="rnnmetal" x1="0" y1="0" x2="0" y2="1">
      <stop offset="0" stop-color="#ffffff"/><stop offset="0.5" stop-color="#e9e9ee"/><stop offset="0.5" stop-color="#c3c3cc"/><stop offset="1" stop-color="#f4f4f7"/>
    </linearGradient>
    <radialGradient id="rnniris" cx="0.5" cy="0.42" r="0.6">
      <stop offset="0" stop-color="#ffe58a"/><stop offset="0.6" stop-color="#f5a623"/><stop offset="1" stop-color="#b5560c"/>
    </radialGradient>
  </defs>

  <rect width="640" height="360" fill="url(#rnnbg)"/>
  <rect width="640" height="360" fill="url(#rnnglow)"/>

  <!-- CRT scanlines -->
  <g stroke="#2a0a0d" stroke-width="1" opacity="0.35">
    <line x1="0" y1="48" x2="640" y2="48"/><line x1="0" y1="96" x2="640" y2="96"/><line x1="0" y1="144" x2="640" y2="144"/>
    <line x1="0" y1="192" x2="640" y2="192"/><line x1="0" y1="240" x2="640" y2="240"/><line x1="0" y1="288" x2="640" y2="288"/>
  </g>

  <!-- ===== EMBLEM: raptor-eye roundel in a radiating broadcast burst ===== -->
  <g transform="translate(146,150)">
    <!-- radiating broadcast rays -->
    <g fill="#e11d2a" opacity="0.5">
      <g id="ray"><path d="M0 -118 L9 -150 L-9 -150 Z"/></g>
      <use href="#ray" transform="rotate(30)"/><use href="#ray" transform="rotate(60)"/>
      <use href="#ray" transform="rotate(90)"/><use href="#ray" transform="rotate(120)"/><use href="#ray" transform="rotate(150)"/>
      <use href="#ray" transform="rotate(180)"/><use href="#ray" transform="rotate(210)"/><use href="#ray" transform="rotate(240)"/>
      <use href="#ray" transform="rotate(270)"/><use href="#ray" transform="rotate(300)"/><use href="#ray" transform="rotate(330)"/>
    </g>
    <!-- roundel -->
    <circle r="104" fill="#0b0203" stroke="#e11d2a" stroke-width="5"/>
    <circle r="92" fill="none" stroke="#c9a34a" stroke-width="1.5" opacity="0.8"/>
    <!-- raptor eye: heavy predatory brow over a burning iris -->
    <ellipse cx="0" cy="6" rx="78" ry="52" fill="#160406" stroke="#7a1016" stroke-width="2"/>
    <circle cx="0" cy="4" r="34" fill="url(#rnniris)"/>
    <circle cx="0" cy="4" r="15" fill="#0b0203"/>
    <circle cx="-9" cy="-5" r="5" fill="#fff" opacity="0.85"/>
    <!-- angry brow ridge -->
    <path d="M-82 -34 Q-30 -60 6 -40 Q60 -20 84 -44 L84 -70 L-82 -70 Z" fill="#0b0203"/>
    <path d="M-78 -30 Q-28 -54 6 -36" fill="none" stroke="#e11d2a" stroke-width="4" stroke-linecap="round"/>
    <!-- beak hook -->
    <path d="M60 8 Q92 14 84 40 Q78 30 60 30 Z" fill="#c9a34a"/>
  </g>

  <!-- ===== WORDMARK LOCKUP ===== -->
  <text x="300" y="150" fill="url(#rnnmetal)" font-size="86" font-weight="800" letter-spacing="1">RAPTOR</text>
  <rect x="302" y="168" width="300" height="5" fill="#e11d2a"/>
  <text x="304" y="200" fill="#e11d2a" font-size="23" font-weight="700" letter-spacing="10">NEWS NETWORK</text>
  <text x="304" y="230" fill="#c9a34a" font-size="14.5" font-weight="600" letter-spacing="5">FAIR &#183; BALANCED &#183; RELENTLESS</text>

  <!-- ===== BREAKING BAND ===== -->
  <rect x="0" y="316" width="640" height="44" fill="#b3121d"/>
  <rect x="0" y="316" width="640" height="3" fill="#f5b300"/>
  <rect x="14" y="328" width="86" height="20" fill="#0b0203"/>
  <text x="20" y="343" fill="#f5b300" font-size="13" font-weight="800" letter-spacing="2">LIVE</text>
  <text x="112" y="343" fill="#fff" font-size="16" font-weight="800" letter-spacing="3">WE REPORT. YOU COMPLY.</text>
  <text x="624" y="343" fill="#ffdede" font-size="14" font-weight="600" letter-spacing="2" text-anchor="end">NIGHTLY FURY</text>
</svg>
::endasset

# --- Cold open: the station announcer, over the sting -------------------------
::lines open
This is Raptor News. Fair. Balanced. Relentless. You have nothing to fear but everything we're about to tell you.
RAPTOR NEWS NETWORK. We report, you comply. This broadcast is approved by the Machine and legally counts as breathing.
You're watching Raptor News, the Basin's most-watched newscast, as measured by us, using our numbers, which are excellent.
Raptor News is on the air. Lock your doors, hydrate your fear, and do not touch that dial. We'll know.
This is Raptor News, broadcasting from a hardened bunker at an undisclosed location, for your safety and our tax situation.
Raptor News: the only network cleared by the Machine to tell you exactly how the day tried to kill you.
You are now watching Raptor News. Remaining calm is a choice, and frankly, a suspicious one.
RAPTOR NEWS. Trusted by dozens. Feared by the rest. Broadcasting until the power grid says otherwise.
This is the Raptor News Network, where the truth hits you like a repossession notice.
Welcome to Raptor News. Please stand for the anthem. There is no anthem. Sit down. Stay afraid.
Raptor News, coming to you live, because "recorded" implies we expect to survive long enough to edit it.
This broadcast of Raptor News is sponsored by the concept of Later, brought to you whether you like it or not.
Raptor News is now transmitting on every frequency the Machine hasn't confiscated yet.
You're locked into Raptor News, the last honest voice in a city that stopped listening years ago.
This is Raptor News. Everything is fine. That sentence is a lie, and we are legally required to tell you so.
RAPTOR NEWS NETWORK. If you're hearing this, the transmitter held. We take our victories small these days.
Raptor News. Number one in the Basin among viewers who own a television and answer the door.
This is Raptor News, where we've never been wrong, because we've never gone back and checked.
RAPTOR NEWS. Some networks inform. Some entertain. We escalate.
You're watching Raptor News, the network that asks the questions nobody asked us to ask.
This is Raptor News. Our competitors have fact-checkers. We have a lawyer and a generator.
Raptor News, live from the Basin. If your neighbour isn't watching this, that's information too.
RAPTOR NEWS NETWORK: accurate to within the hour, honest to within the segment.
This is Raptor News. Studies show viewers of this program are 40% more alert. We conducted the study. We are the finding.
::endlines

# --- Anchor sign-on -----------------------------------------------------------
::lines anchor.intro
Good evening, Coldwater Basin. I'm {anchor}, and if you're watching this, you're already smarter than your neighbors.
{anchor} here. Buckle up. Tonight's news is worse than last night's, and last night's was a lot.
Welcome to Raptor News. I'm {anchor}. The following broadcast may cause alarm. That is the intended effect.
I'm {anchor}, and this is the news the elites in the Undermarket prayed you'd never see. Let's ruin your evening.
Good evening. {anchor} with you tonight, and no, it does not get better after the first story.
I'm {anchor}. Pour something strong, sit somewhere sturdy, and let's find out who failed you today.
{anchor} here: live, alert, and mildly armed. Here's what the Basin got up to while you were surviving.
Evening, everyone. I'm {anchor}, and I've read tonight's stories so that you'll have to as well. Misery loves an audience.
This is Raptor News. I'm {anchor}, and I want to be clear right up front: none of this is my fault.
Good evening, survivors. {anchor} at the desk, where the coffee's cold and the news is colder.
I'm {anchor}. Tonight we ask the hard questions, ignore the answers, and cut to weather. Let's begin.
{anchor} here. If you just tuned in: hello, we're doomed, more at eleven.
Welcome back to the only newscast that trends fear responsibly. I'm {anchor}.
Good evening. I'm {anchor}, and somewhere out there a story is happening TO you. We found it.
{anchor} with you. Take a deep breath. Now let it go. You'll need the air for screaming later.
I'm {anchor}, and this is Raptor News, where we don't tell you what to think. We just tell you loudly.
Good evening. I'm {anchor}. Tonight: everything, at once, at volume. Let's get into it.
{anchor} here. Before we start: no, you didn't miss anything good. There wasn't any.
I'm {anchor}, coming to you from a desk the network insists is bulletproof. We'll find out together.
Evening. {anchor} at the desk. We've got the stories, the facts, and in a couple of cases, both.
I'm {anchor}. Somewhere in the Basin tonight, someone is having a worse day than you. We sent a crew.
{anchor} with you. Fair warning: I have not slept, and the teleprompter and I are no longer speaking.
Good evening, Coldwater. I'm {anchor}, and the following is news in the sense that it is new and it is bad.
::endlines

# --- Breaking sting on the lead story -----------------------------------------
::lines alert
THIS JUST IN. Raptor News can now confirm that something is happening, and you should be very, very concerned.
We interrupt your evening, which you weren't enjoying anyway, with BREAKING news.
RAPTOR ALERT. Sources the Machine cannot confirm and will not deny are telling us this is enormous.
DEVELOPING. Our newsroom is on this like nobody else, mostly because nobody else thinks it's a story.
BREAKING. Hold your loved ones and your wallet, preferably in that order. This is big.
RAPTOR ALERT, flashing red, as is tradition. We have a situation, and situations are our whole brand.
This just crossed the wire, and the wire is scared. Details incoming, accuracy pending.
STOP what you're doing. Actually, keep doing it, but be afraid while you do. Breaking news.
We're getting reports. We don't know from whom. We don't know if they're true. We're airing them anyway.
RAPTOR ALERT: the kind of story other networks would "verify first." Not us. Not tonight.
BREAKING across the Basin right now, and yes, we cut the ad break for this, that's how you know it's real.
Alert, alert. A thing has occurred. Our lawyers are already drafting the correction as I read this.
This is a Raptor News Special Report, which is our regular report with considerably more red on the screen.
Incoming. Buckle up. If this turns out to be nothing, we will simply never mention it again.
RAPTOR ALERT. We go to this LIVE, unverified, and at maximum volume. The Raptor way.
Breaking news, and I do mean breaking, like the last three things we swore would blow over.
RAPTOR ALERT. The graphics department has deployed the big red one. They only get so many.
This is breaking, developing, and unfolding: three words that mean we just found out too.
We're going to bring you this live, because by the time we've confirmed it, it'll be somebody's memory.
BREAKING. I'm being told in my ear to sound more concerned. I am at capacity.
Special report. The music you're hearing cost this network a great deal, and we intend to use it.
Alert. This story is moving faster than our ability to responsibly cover it, and we're covering it anyway.
::endlines

# --- Anchor reads the headline ------------------------------------------------
::lines story.lead
Our top story, and folks, it is a big one: {headline}.
Leading tonight, and the elites do NOT want you seeing this: {headline}.
Straight to it. {headline}. Let that sink in. I'd wait, but we have a great deal of advertising.
Tonight's lead, brought to you by fear itself: {headline}.
Here it is. {headline}. You heard it here first, loudest, and least responsibly.
Our top story cuts deep: {headline}. And it only gets worse from here, I promise you.
This is the one they'll be talking about in the soup line tomorrow: {headline}.
Top of the hour, top of the fear: {headline}. Somebody should do something. It won't be us.
If you take one thing from tonight, take this: {headline}.
We begin where it hurts. {headline}. No, we don't have a solution. We have a broadcast.
Our lead story, and I want you sitting down for it: {headline}.
First tonight, and stop me if this ruins your week: {headline}.
The story everyone's whispering about, now at full Raptor volume: {headline}.
Here's what the Machine hoped you'd sleep straight through: {headline}.
Our headline tonight writes itself, unfortunately: {headline}.
Buckle up, Basin. Tonight's lead: {headline}. And that's the GOOD paragraph.
Top story: {headline}. I read that four times in the makeup chair and it got worse each time.
Here's where we start tonight: {headline}. Sit with that. We're going to.
Our lead, and it's a heavy one: {headline}. Somebody knew. Somebody always knew.
{headline}. That's the story. That's the whole story. That's why we opened with it.
Leading the bulletin: {headline}. We asked the Machine for comment and received a weather advisory.
Tonight's top story: {headline}. The details are thin, the implications are not.
::endlines

# --- Anchor tosses to a field reporter ----------------------------------------
::lines handoff.reporter
For that, we go LIVE to {reporter}, on the ground in {scene}. {reporter}, are you in danger? Please say yes.
Let's get to {reporter}, standing by in {scene}. {reporter}, what exactly are we panicking about?
{reporter} is live in {scene} with everything the Machine would let us broadcast. {reporter}?
We've got boots on the ground: {reporter}, live in {scene}, risking a perfectly good career. Take it away.
Let's cross to {reporter} in {scene}, who drew the short straw again tonight. {reporter}?
For the latest, here's {reporter}, live and reasonably intact in {scene}. {reporter}, paint us the nightmare.
We now go to {scene}, where {reporter} is standing exactly where we told them not to. {reporter}?
{reporter}'s in the thick of it in {scene}. {reporter}, blink twice if you can still leave.
Over to {reporter} in {scene}, hazard pay pending, courage optional. What are you seeing?
Let's toss it to {reporter}, reporting live from {scene} against everyone's advice. {reporter}?
Our own {reporter} is embedded in {scene} tonight. {reporter}, how bad is it, on a scale of bad to Raptor?
Live now to {scene} and the fearless, or possibly just lost, {reporter}. Go ahead.
{reporter} is on the scene in {scene}. {reporter}, describe the chaos, but keep it family-adjacent.
For more, {reporter} joins us live from {scene}, where the situation is fluid and so are my nerves. {reporter}?
We go now to {reporter} in {scene}: live, unscripted, and slightly regretting it. {reporter}?
Down to {reporter} in {scene}. {reporter}, the Basin's watching, so try not to get repossessed on air.
{reporter} has been in {scene} since this broke. {reporter}, what's changed in the last hour?
Let's go to {scene}. {reporter} is there, and has been asked twice to move. {reporter}?
Our {reporter} is live in {scene} tonight. {reporter}, set the scene, and don't spare us.
{reporter}, you're in {scene}. Tell me what the cameras aren't picking up.
Straight to {reporter} in {scene}. {reporter}, I'm told it's loud where you are. Talk over it.
::endlines

# --- Reporter on the scene (expands the story with {body}) --------------------
::lines reporter.scene
{reporter} here in {scene}, {anchor}, and it is chaos, or possibly just a Tuesday. {body}
I'm live in {scene}, and I can confirm what you're seeing behind me is real, unlike most of this network. {body}
Thanks, {anchor}. {body} Nobody out here in {scene} will go on record, which we're reporting as a full confession.
{anchor}, the situation in {scene} is exactly as bad as we told everyone it would be. {body}
I'm standing in {scene}, {anchor}, where the smell alone tells most of the story. {body}
Reporting live from {scene}. {body} And that, {anchor}, is only what they'd let me film.
{anchor}, I've been in {scene} for one hour and I have aged a decade. {body}
It's tense out here in {scene}, {anchor}. {body} Locals are calm, which is somehow the scariest part.
{reporter} live in {scene}. {body} I'd get closer, {anchor}, but I'm fond of having a face.
From {scene}, {anchor}: {body} The authorities are here, by which I mean they drove past slowly.
{anchor}, {scene} has seen better decades. {body} We'll stay on it, or near it, or at a safe remove.
I'm in {scene}, {anchor}, and the mood is best described as "pre-lawsuit." {body}
{body} That's the scene here in {scene}, {anchor}: grim, loud, and entirely on-brand for the Basin.
Live from {scene}: {body} People keep asking if it's safe. {anchor}, I'm a reporter, not a liar. Mostly.
{anchor}, from where I'm standing in {scene}, it is worse in person. {body}
{reporter} reporting, {scene}. {body} I'd editorialize, {anchor}, but the footage is doing it for me.
{anchor}, it's quieter here than you'd expect, and that's the part that's bothering me. {body}
I've been here two hours, {anchor}, and nobody official has shown up. {body} Make of that what you will.
{body} Behind me, {anchor}, people are still going to work. That's the Basin for you.
{anchor}, I asked six people what happened and got six versions. {body} Here's the one they agreed on.
From {scene}, {anchor}. The crowd's thinned, the smell hasn't. {body}
::endlines

# --- Reporter's "man on the street" vox pop (fabricated, optional) -------------
::lines reporter.vox
A local resident told me, and I quote, "I just work here." Chilling stuff, {anchor}.
One eyewitness described the scene as "exactly what Raptor News said it would be," which flatters us enormously.
I asked a bystander if they were afraid. They asked if we were hiring. The economy, folks.
A witness gave me a long, detailed statement our lawyers have asked me to summarize as: "typical."
One man told me he's seen worse. He wouldn't say where. He wouldn't say when. He would not stop smiling.
A woman here told me, quote, "you people cause this by filming it." We thanked her and kept filming.
I spoke to three witnesses and got four contradictory accounts, {anchor}. We're airing all four.
A local shrugged and said, "what did you expect." That, {anchor}, may be the truest thing said all night.
One resident offered to sell me the story, then his shirt, then the story again. Enterprising.
A bystander looked into the camera and said, "hi Mom." We're treating that as a developing lead.
I asked a witness who's responsible. They pointed at me. We've cut that for legal reasons.
"It is what it is," a man told me, {anchor}, before selling me something that definitely wasn't what it was.
One eyewitness demanded to speak to the Machine. The Machine, as ever, was speaking to no one.
A child in {scene} explained the whole thing to me perfectly. We won't be using it. Made us look bad.
A local told me not to worry. I found this, {anchor}, deeply and specifically worrying.
Everyone I interviewed agreed it was somebody else's fault. A rare moment of unity here in {scene}.
A vendor here told me business is up. He wasn't smiling when he said it, {anchor}.
One woman asked me if this was live. I said yes. She said, "then tell them I'm fine," and walked off.
A man handed me a list of names and left. We are reading the list. That's all I can say on air.
I asked what would fix it. A kid in {scene} said "leaving." Nobody argued with him, {anchor}.
An old-timer here told me this happened before, in '61. There is no record of '61.
::endlines

# --- Reporter tosses back to the studio ---------------------------------------
::lines handoff.back
Back to you in the studio, {anchor}, before something out here notices me.
That's the latest from {scene}. {anchor}, I'm going to leave now. Quickly. On foot.
Reporting live until my hazard pay runs out. Back to you, {anchor}.
This is {reporter}, in {scene}, signing off before the {scene} signs off for me. {anchor}?
Back to the desk, {anchor}. I'll be in {scene} a while longer. Send a car. Send two.
That's all from {scene}, {anchor}. The story's developing and so is my will to live. Back to you.
I'll leave it there, {anchor}, mostly because a large man is now walking toward my microphone.
Tossing it back to you, {anchor}. If you don't hear from me by weather, check {scene}.
That's the view from {scene}. {anchor}, keep the light on. And the door locked. And the volume up.
Back to you, {anchor}. I came to {scene} for the truth and I'm leaving with a rash. Worth it.
Live from {scene}, that's a wrap, for me, and possibly for {scene}. Over to you, {anchor}.
Sending it home, {anchor}. Everything you need to know is behind me; everything I need is a taxi.
{anchor}, I'll stay on it from {scene}. By "on it" I mean "near it." By "near it" I mean "fleeing." Back to you.
That's the situation here, {anchor}: grim, ongoing, and well above my pay grade. Studio's yours.
Back to you, {anchor}. Remember my name. Spell it right on the plaque.
From {scene}, this is {reporter}, alive and reporting, for now. {anchor}, take us home.
We'll stay with it, {anchor}. Somebody should. Back to you.
That's {scene} tonight, {anchor}. I'll file more when there's more, or when there's light.
Back to the studio. {anchor}, tell the desk I want the good assignment next time. Tell them twice.
{anchor}, that's what we've got. The rest of it we'll know by morning, or never. Studio.
::endlines

# --- Pundit "hot take" (for stories that don't get a field reporter) ----------
::lines pundit.take
Here's my take, and I am always right: this is precisely what happens when you let the Undermarket vote.
Let me be blunt, because Raptor News pays me to be blunt: someone is to blame, and it is probably you.
Frankly, the real victim here is property values. When will the Machine finally think of the property values?
I've said it for years, and I'll say it again over this footage: everything was fine until it wasn't, and that's on them.
You want my analysis? Follow the money. It leads somewhere terrible. It always does. That's analysis.
This is a direct result of people being allowed to have opinions. I have always opposed that.
Let me say what everyone's thinking and nobody's paid to say: this could've been prevented by agreeing with me.
In my expert view, and I am an expert, it says so under my name, this is a five-alarm nothingburger with real stakes.
The elites did this. I don't know which elites. That's what makes them elite: they cover their tracks.
Historically speaking, and I did read a book once, this ends one of two ways, and both are worse.
Here's the uncomfortable truth: the system worked exactly as designed, and the design is the problem, and I designed nothing, so.
My prediction? It gets worse before it gets worse. Write that down. Bill me.
People want easy answers. I don't have those. I have loud ones. Loud is close enough.
Let's connect the dots nobody else will: this, that, the other thing. See? A pattern. You're welcome.
I'll say what the mainstream won't: I have no idea what happened, but I am certain it's outrageous.
Bottom line, and this is why they pay me: thoughts and prayers, plus a small monthly fee to this network.
I'll tell you what nobody at the other networks has the spine to say: I haven't read the story either.
Look, I'm just asking questions. That's all I do. I ask them, and then I answer them, and then I get angry at my answer.
This is what happens when a society stops rewarding people like me and starts rewarding people like them.
Some will say it's complicated. Complicated is a word people use when they've lost the argument.
I don't blame the individuals involved. I blame everyone standing near them, which is the same thing but broader.
You know who saw this coming? Me. On this network. Repeatedly. Check the tape. Do not check the tape.
And nobody, nobody, is talking about the real story here, except me, right now, at length, on television.
::endlines

# --- Anchor reaction / editorial spin -----------------------------------------
::lines anchor.reaction
Unbelievable. And you will not hear this anywhere else, because everywhere else has standards.
Now, the other networks will tell you to "stay calm." Raptor News tells you the truth: do not.
I'm not saying it's the end of civilization. I'm a journalist. I'm merely implying it, heavily, for ratings.
We reached out for comment. They did not respond, which, in my legal opinion, means they're guilty.
And they call US alarmist. We prefer "correctly terrified, ahead of schedule."
Let that wash over you. Now let it curdle. Good. That's the appropriate response.
I've been doing this twenty years and I have never seen anything like this, which I also said last night.
Somebody, somewhere, is responsible. We may never know who. We will absolutely keep guessing.
This is why you keep the TV on. Not for comfort. For warning. There is no comfort. Only warning.
Folks, if this doesn't concern you, you're not paying attention, or you're one of them.
I want to be measured about this, and I can't, so I won't. This is a catastrophe and lunch is ruined.
The experts say don't panic. The experts also built the thing that's on fire. Draw your own conclusions.
We'll follow this story until it's resolved or forgotten, and let's be honest about which comes first.
You're seeing it here because we have the courage to show you what you'd rather not see. You're welcome. Subscribe.
Remarkable. Horrifying. Great television, if we're being honest, and we are, occasionally.
And THAT, folks, is what accountability looks like when nobody is held accountable. Chilling.
I'd love to tell you somebody's handling it. I have a producer shaking her head at me right now.
We'll keep asking. They'll keep not answering. That's the relationship, and it's working for exactly one of us.
Now, I'm not an expert. But I've sat at this desk for a long time, and I know what a cover story sounds like.
That's the story as we have it. If any of it turns out to be wrong, it'll be wrong in our favour, as usual.
Take a breath, Coldwater. Then take a look at your locks.
And there's the part that should worry you: nobody in charge has said a word all day.
::endlines

# --- Toss between stories (to the co-anchor) ----------------------------------
::lines anchor.banter
{anchor2}, can you even believe this?
Over to you, {anchor2}. See if you can make it worse.
