# =============================================================================
# THE RAPTOR FURY WEATHER DECK — the bulletin's weather segment.
# These are NOT decoration: the server fills them from the SAME live 7-day forecast
# DOOMCAST reads, so {temp}, {wind}, {precip} and the pool that gets picked are the
# real sky over the Basin tonight. Skip names a hero day — acid, ion — as itself,
# because a warning that arrives as a joke is still the only warning anyone gets.
# Tokens here: {meteorologist} {weather} {temp} {feels} {wind} {windLabel} {humidity}
#              {precip} {day} {season} {hiTemp} {loTemp} {severeCount} {worstDay}
# =============================================================================

# --- Anchor tosses to the weather deck ----------------------------------------
::lines wx.toss
Now to the Raptor Fury Weather Deck, where {meteorologist} has been shouting at a map since noon. {meteorologist}?
Let's check the sky, which has been openly hostile all week. {meteorologist}, what's it planning?
{meteorologist}'s at the weather wall with a forecast our lawyers have described as "actionable." Take it.
Time for weather, the only segment on this network where the threats are verified. {meteorologist}?
Over to {meteorologist} at the Fury Deck. {meteorologist}, give it to us with both barrels.
{meteorologist}, you've had the radar all day and the look on your face all evening. Go ahead.
Weather now, and I'm told you should be sitting down for it. {meteorologist}?
Let's bring in {meteorologist}, who has never once told me good news. {meteorologist}.
::endlines

# --- Weathercaster sign-on -----------------------------------------------------
::lines wx.open
Thanks, {anchor}. I've been staring at this system for six hours and it has not blinked.
{anchor}, the radar's lit up like a citation. Here's what's actually over your head.
Good evening. I'm {meteorologist}, and unlike the rest of this broadcast, I have instruments.
Thanks, {anchor}. Fair warning: the atmosphere did not consult us on any of this.
Here's your sky, {anchor}, and it is a {season} sky with something to prove.
Appreciate it, {anchor}. Numbers first, panic after. That's the deal at this desk.
::endlines

# --- Conditions by sky (mirrors DOOMCAST's pools, in Skip's voice) -------------
::lines wx.sky.clear
Clear out there, {temp} degrees and not a cloud in it. Enjoy it, and then ask yourself why it's free.
Wide open sky tonight, {temp} degrees. Clear is not the same as safe, but I'll take it.
::endlines

::lines wx.sky.fog
Fog's in, {humidity}% humidity and visibility down to whoever's already standing next to you.
Thick fog over the Basin. {temp} degrees, and the city's gone from the map for a few hours.
::endlines

::lines wx.sky.haze
Haze across the whole Basin. {temp} degrees of flat grey air you can taste on the back of your teeth.
It's hazy out. The skyline's a rumour and the air's got texture. Breathe shallow.
::endlines

::lines wx.sky.cloudy
Cloud cover, {temp} degrees, sky can't commit. Neither can anyone else this week.
Partly cloudy tonight at {temp}. Unremarkable, which around here is practically a holiday.
::endlines

::lines wx.sky.overcast
Full overcast, {temp} degrees under a lid. No sky tonight, just ceiling.
Solid grey top to bottom, {temp} degrees. The sun's filed for absence and nobody's chasing it.
::endlines

::lines wx.sky.rain
Rain, {precip}% of it, {temp} degrees. It's wet, it's dirty, and it's going straight down your collar.
{precip}% chance of rain and I'd take that bet. Waterproof whatever you can't replace.
::endlines

::lines wx.sky.sleet
Sleet: {temp} degrees, half rain, half ice, coming in sideways. Nobody's dressed for this.
It's sleeting out there. {temp} degrees and every surface in the Basin is now a decision.
::endlines

::lines wx.sky.snow
Snow, {temp} degrees, settling on anything that stops moving. Don't stop moving.
It's snowing across the Basin, {temp} degrees. Beautiful from a window. Lethal from a doorway.
::endlines

::lines wx.sky.thunderstorm
Thunderstorms, {precip}% chance, {temp} degrees. The sky's picking targets and it isn't picky.
Lightning's in play tonight. If you can count the gap to the thunder, you are the tallest thing you know.
::endlines

::lines wx.sky.blizzard
Blizzard conditions: {temp} degrees, feels like {feels}, {wind} km/h of it in your face.
Whiteout out there. {feels}-degree windchill. If you can see this screen, stay exactly where you are.
::endlines

::lines wx.sky.storm
Storm-force, {wind} km/h, {temp} degrees. The grid's holding its breath and so am I.
Full system on top of us: wind, water, and a power company that has already stopped answering.
::endlines

# --- Hero events: acid and ion. The joke stops here. --------------------------
::lines wx.sky.acid
Acid. Not bad rain. Acid. {precip}% chance, and it goes through a coat and then through you.
The sky's gone yellow and it means it. Sealed rainwear or four walls. There is no third option, {anchor}.
Caustic rain over the Basin. I'll say it plainly because this network rarely does: do not go out in it.
::endlines

::lines wx.sky.ion
Ion storm. The air goes conductive, the grid goes down, and anything with a circuit in it becomes scrap.
Static's building to a scream out there. Charge nothing, trust nothing with a battery, sit down if you're chromed.
Pulse conditions across the Basin. When the lights go, they go all at once, and yes, that includes us.
::endlines

# --- Warnings (severity band, or a hero day) ----------------------------------
::lines wx.warn.cold
⚠ Cold warning: {temp} degrees, feels like {feels}. Thermal gear tonight, or a very short walk.
⚠ {day} runs to {temp}. That's not discomfort, that's exposure. Layer or don't go.
::endlines

::lines wx.warn.heat
⚠ Heat warning: {temp} degrees. Shade, water, and nothing heroic between noon and four.
⚠ {day} bakes at {temp}. Heatstroke's fast and free. Decline it.
::endlines

::lines wx.warn.wind
⚠ Wind warning, {wind} km/h, {windLabel}. Off the rooftops, away from the glass.
⚠ {day}: gusts to {wind}. If it can fall on you, tonight it will.
::endlines

::lines wx.warn.blizzard
⚠ Blizzard warning for {day}. {feels}-degree windchill. This one takes stragglers.
⚠ Severe: {day} is a killer. Thermal gear or a will, {anchor}. Your viewers pick one.
::endlines

::lines wx.warn.storm
⚠ Storm warning, {day}. Lightning, {wind} km/h, and a grid that may not hold. Charge what you can now.
⚠ {day} brings the whole system. Stay in, stay off the roofs, and keep a light that doesn't need power.
::endlines

::lines wx.warn.acid
⚠⚠ ACID WARNING, {day}. This is the one that takes skin off. Sealed rainwear or you stay indoors.
⚠⚠ Caustic front {day}. It eats coats, it eats gear, then it starts on you. Cover every inch.
⚠⚠ {day} rains acid, {anchor}. I'm not being colourful for the ratings. That's a chemical burn falling out of the sky.
::endlines

::lines wx.warn.ion
⚠⚠ ION STORM {day}. The grid drops and stays down. Anything electronic on you gets cooked.
⚠⚠ Pulse warning for {day}. Leave the deck home, shield the tablet, and if you're chromed, be sitting down.
⚠⚠ Lights out citywide {day}. Torch that doesn't need a circuit, room you trust. You've had the notice.
::endlines

::lines wx.warn.generic
⚠ The numbers on {day} come in severe, {anchor}, and the numbers don't flatter anybody.
⚠ Flagging {day} as severe. Not for drama. For the record, and for whoever's listening.
::endlines

# --- Week arc ------------------------------------------------------------------
::lines wx.trend.warming
It climbs from here: {loTemp} tonight to {hiTemp} by week's end. Enjoy it suspiciously.
Warming trend, {hiTemp} degrees waiting at the far side of the week.
::endlines

::lines wx.trend.cooling
We shed it fast: {hiTemp} down to {loTemp} before the week's out. Dig out the layers now.
Cooling all week, bottoming at {loTemp}. Whatever heat you've got, ration it.
::endlines

::lines wx.trend.clearing
Good news, and I hate saying it on this network: the wet clears as the week runs on.
It dries out toward the weekend. Briefly. Don't build a life on it.
::endlines

::lines wx.trend.deteriorating
It gets worse. {severeCount} severe days ahead, {worstDay} the worst of them. Plan around it or pay for it.
{severeCount} days flagged severe this week, {anchor}. {worstDay} is the one I'd move things off.
::endlines

::lines wx.trend.steady
Flat all week: same grey, same grind, {loTemp} to {hiTemp} and no surprises. Consistency, at least.
No real movement ahead. The sky's found its rut and it's staying in it.
::endlines

# --- Weathercaster tosses back --------------------------------------------------
::lines wx.back
That's your sky, {anchor}. Dress for the worst number I said, not the one you liked.
Back to you, {anchor}. The forecast's free; ignoring it costs.
That's the Fury Deck. I'll be here, {anchor}, watching it come.
Numbers are the numbers, {anchor}. Do with them what the rest of this show never does.
::endlines

